# XMTC Propensity Robustness Toolkit

This repository implements the experimental core for the project
"Robustez de Metricas com Propensity Scoring em Extreme Multi-Label
Classification".

The code is organized around interchangeable rankers and a metric engine so the
research question can be tested directly:

> Are propensity-scored metrics robust against a naive global-frequency baseline
> under highly imbalanced XMTC label distributions?

## Main components

- `GlobalFrequencyRanker`: ignores text and always predicts the most frequent
  training labels.
- `InstanceKNNRetrievalRanker`: embeds documents, retrieves nearest training
  instances with FAISS, HNSWlib, or scikit-learn, then aggregates neighbor
  labels.
- `metrics`: implements `Precision@k`, `nDCG@k`, `PS-Precision@k`, and
  `PS-nDCG@k` with Jain et al. propensity scores.
- `analysis`: evaluates head/tail label partitions and sensitivity to label
  frequency.

## Install

```bash
pip install -e .
pip install -e ".[faiss,embeddings]"
```

Use the base install for TF-IDF plus scikit-learn. Add `faiss` or `hnsw` for
large dense indexes. Add `embeddings` for Sentence-Transformers encoders.

## Minimal usage

```python
from xmtc_robustness import (
    GlobalFrequencyRanker,
    InstanceKNNRetrievalRanker,
    evaluate_predictions,
    inverse_propensity_scores,
)

baseline = GlobalFrequencyRanker().fit(y_train)
baseline_pred = baseline.predict_top_k(n_samples=y_test.shape[0], k=5)

retriever = InstanceKNNRetrievalRanker(
    representation="tfidf",
    backend="sklearn",
    n_neighbors=32,
).fit(train_texts, y_train)
retriever_pred = retriever.predict_top_k(test_texts, k=5)

inv_ps = inverse_propensity_scores.from_label_matrix(
    y_train,
    dataset="amazon-670k",
)

print(evaluate_predictions(y_test, baseline_pred, ks=(1, 3, 5), inverse_propensity=inv_ps))
print(evaluate_predictions(y_test, retriever_pred, ks=(1, 3, 5), inverse_propensity=inv_ps))
```
